   "At COP31, governments back action on extreme heat and sustainable cooling | UNEP - UN Environment Programme Skip to main content Utility Knowledge repository Data and apps Language English Search Image search Go to search menu close Open main menu Main navigation Who we are About us Meetings and governance Evaluation, policies and strategies Funding Data hubs Platforms and partnerships Contact us Where we work Africa Asia and the Pacific Europe Latin America and the Caribbean North America and New York Office West Asia What we do Climate action Nature and land action Chemicals and pollution action Air quality Biosafety Cities Digital transformations Disasters and conflicts Energy Environment under review Environmental law and governance Extractives Finance and economic transformations Food systems Forests Fresh water Gender Ocean, seas and coasts Peatlands Sustainable Development Goals Transport Youth, education and environment What s new News, stories & speeches Thematic feeds Publications Events Get involved Agenda 2030 Breadcrumb Home News, stories & speeches Press releases At COP31, governments back action on extreme heat and sustainable cooling Title At COP31, governments back action on extreme heat and sustainable cooling Main image Image Image credit Photo Credit: Kaden Taylor/Unsplash Published on 17 Nov 2026 Body As global temperatures rise, cooling must expand to reach nearly three billion people who could lack access by 2050, while exclusive reliance on air conditioning could drive emissions to 7.2 billion tonnes of CO₂e and strain electricity grids. Singapore, Brazil, Japan, and the United Kingdom commit to addressing both extreme heat and sustainable cooling through the Global Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap Over US$ 20 million in new support announced to accelerate sustainable cooling, strengthen heat resilience and implement the Global Cooling Pledge. Anatalya, 17 November 2026 – Countries attending the UN Climate Change Conference (COP31) in Antalya, Türkiye, today backed an integrated global response to extreme heat and growing cooling needs, welcoming the Antalya Communiqué and Global Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap to protect people as global temperature rise gets set to exceed 1.5°C. Meeting at the 3 rd Cooling Pledge Ministerial , co-chaired by Singapore and the UN Environment Programme (UNEP), ministers and high-level representatives outlined priorities for translating global commitments into action, including strengthening national and local cooling plans, scaling sustainable cooling solutions and mobilizing finance. Countries and partners also announced over US$ 20 million in new support to accelerate implementation of the Global Cooling Pledge. “Nearly two billion people, globally, still lack adequate cooling,” said Inger Andersen, Executive Director of the UN Environment Programme (UNEP). “At the same time, growing demand for cooling is placing unprecedented pressure on electricity systems and contributing to greenhouse gas emissions. “We need to reduce heat exposure and cooling demand through better planning, shade, trees and building design, while ensuring access to efficient, affordable and sustainable cooling and cleaner refrigeration,” she added. “The Communiqué sets the momentum, and the roadmap will help countries bring these solutions together.” The Antalya Communiqué The Antalya Communiqué sets out the next steps to bring together heat resilience and sustainable cooling. It aligns countries and partners around five priority areas where collective action, policy, finance, technology can transform markets and translate the Global Cooling Pledge in delivery for both heat resilience and sustainable cooling. It builds on the outcomes and priority action areas of the first Global Cooling Pledge Assembly in Singapore and includes developing a Global Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap ; advancing national and subnational cooling action plans; scaling up passive, nature-based, efficient and innovative cooling solutions; mobilizing finance, including through Article 6 of the Paris Agreement ; and strengthening the science, political coordination, and international advocacy needed to accelerate implementation. The Roadmap Countries want to take the next step in dealing with extreme heat and boosting sustainable cooling. What they need now is a clear picture of heat risk and cooling demand: who is most exposed, which measures will protect them, and what those measures mean for the power grid and the climate. The Roadmap is that step. The roadmap is a country-led and voluntary process, which will support countries in identifying the people, places and systems most exposed to extreme heat – assessing institutions and policy gaps, prioritising solutions suited to national circumstances, and identifying pathways to finance and implement them. “No country can tackle rising heat alone. Singapore is committed to build on the momentum from the Global Cooling Pledge Assembly held in September this year to drive global action on heat and cooling,” said Grace Fu, Singapore’s Minister for Sustainability and the Environment. “Through our upcoming ASEAN Chairmanship and our role as a Cool Leader globally, we will work with partners to share practical solutions, strengthen cooperation, and develop cooler and more resilient cities. This includes working with UNEP Cool Coalition and over 200 international partners to help shape the Global Heat Resilience & Sustainable Cooling Roadmap.” At the ministerial, leaders emphasized the need to build on the science of the Global Cooling Watch report and the commitments made through the Global Cooling Pledge and Beat the Heat to make heat resilience and sustainable action an investment and action priority. Countries and partners announced over US$20 million in new funding support to the UNEP-led Cool Coalition to accelerate implementation of the Global Cooling Pledge. This includes expanded support through EPIC Deploy (Enabling Pledge Implementation for Cooling ), supported by Italy’s Ministry of the Environment and Energy Security (MASE) , a UNEP initiative helping countries implement their cooling commitments by developing investment-ready projects and mobilizing finance to scale sustainable cooling solutions. Additional funding will support the adoption of efficient cooling technologies using climate-friendly refrigerants in major markets across the Global South, including Brazil, China, ASEAN and South Asia. These efforts will connect large-scale aggregated demand with manufacturers through the UNEP Cool Coalition Manufacturers Platform. Additional funding was also announced to strengthen national and subnational implementation of the Global Cooling Pledge commitments in signatory countries and cities and to advance heat resilience and sustainable cooling in the Horn of Africa. Funding partners include the Children’s Investment Fund Foundation (CIFF), ClimateWorks Foundation, the Hewlett Foundation, the Sequoia Climate Foundation, the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC) and the UN Development Account. To sustain momentum beyond COP31, Japan announced that it will host the 2028 GlobalABC Annual Assembly and Global Cooling Pledge Assembly in Tokyo as a joint “COOL + BUILD” gathering. Bringing together the buildings and cooling communities, the event will strengthen international cooperation to accelerate the transition to energy-efficient, low-carbon and climate-resilient buildings, while expanding access to sustainable cooling and protecting people from extreme heat. NOTES TO EDITORS About the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) The UN Environment Programme is the leading global voice on the environment. It provides leadership and encourages partnership in caring for the environment by inspiring, informing, and enabling nations and peoples to improve their quality of life without compromising that of future generations. About the UNEP Cool Coalition Launched as a flagship initiative of the UN Secretary-General’s Climate Action Summit in 2019, the UNEP Cool Coalition is a global platform bringing together over 400 governments, businesses, cities, and financial institutions to drive comprehensive action on sustainable cooling and extreme heat. The Cool Coalition serves as Secretariat to the Global Cooling Pledge. About the Global Cooling Pledge The Global Cooling Pledge is the world’s first collective commitment to cut cooling-related emissions by 68% by 2050, while rapidly expanding access to sustainable cooling. It was launched in 2023 by the UNEP Cool Coalition and the COP28 Presidency of Dubai. For more information please contact: News and Media Unit , UN Environment Programme Category Press release Topics Climate action SDG tags Image Goal 13: Climate Action (opens in a new tab) Goal 13: Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts Further Resources Global Cooling Pledge Related content Speech Image 17 Nov 2026 The point of return from 1.5°C Despite missing the chance to stay below 1.5°C warming, rapid emissions cuts and carbon removal can still limit overshoot and return temperatures to safer levels, says Inger Andersen, UNEP Executive Director. Speech Image 17 Nov 2026 A strong current of ambition on the water cycle Water security is central to climate resilience, demanding greater investment in nature-based solutions and sustainable water management. - Inger Andersen, UNEP Executive Director Speech Image 17 Nov 2026 Delivering reductions in non-CO2 gases for overshoot return UNEP Executive Director Inger Andersen: China can drive global climate progress by reducing methane and SF6 emissions, advancing clean technologies and strengthening international partnerships. Footer About us History Structure and leadership Funding FAQs Con"
