   "imate, development and resilience issue. This is a Coalition of the willing and the acting: over 55 National Cooling Action Plans are being developed or implemented, and cooling has been integrated into over nearly 60 Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs). From Brazil to India, finance is being unlocked, industry is acting, efficiency is being improved, nature is being deployed, and hundreds of millions of people are being protected from extreme heat. But, excellencies, as the heat dials up a notch, so must our efforts. The Singapore Assembly delivered a boost with broad recognition that heat resilience and cooling must be addressed together and a growing focus on delivery, investment and partnerships. Here, let me thank the governments of Denmark, Italy, Switzerland, the United Kingdom and the many philanthropies for their partnership and ongoing support. The Assembly saw the launch of the Manufacturing Platform, bringing together governments, manufacturers and buyers to increase the availability of efficient and low- and ultra-low-Global Warming Potential refrigerants cooling equipment. Countries also demanded implementation support. The Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap can become the implementation framework countries want. Countries have repeatedly identified fragmentation as a major obstacle to delivery. Responses remain spread across institutions, sectors and financing streams when they must be coordinated to deal with heat that respects neither boundaries nor institutions. The country-driven Roadmap would offer a practical response: bringing heat resilience and sustainable cooling into a common framework and guiding investments. Together with the Antalya-to-Addis Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Declaration, the Roadmap can support the next phase of Paris Agreement delivery – helping to embed heat resilience and sustainable cooling across existing processes and programmes, the second Global Stocktake (GST), the Global Goal on Adaptation, the Mitigation Work Programme, the Just Transition Work Programme and NDCs. So, excellencies, today I call on you to support the Antalya-to-Addis Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Declaration and send a political signal that heat resilience has become a development, resilience and climate priority. I call on you to shape the Roadmap so that we can identify where heat risks are greatest; select and sequence responses; and connect existing plans, commitments and investments to deliver its three core outcomes: thermal safety for people, heat-resilient systems and climate-compatible delivery. And I call on you to embed heat resilience and sustainable cooling across the Paris implementation agenda as a practical pathway for translating climate commitments into real-world results. Excellencies, The heat is on, but we must remain coolheaded. Make the rational choices based on the science. Ensure our cooling systems are sustainable and available to all. And build the resilience our societies need until we can turn the heat back down. Category Speech Footer About us History Structure and leadership Funding FAQs Contact us Meetings and governance UN Environment Assembly Committee of Permanent Representatives Intergovernmental Negotiating Committee on Plastic Pollution Secretariats and conventions Resources Knowledge repository Annual Report Medium-term Strategy EnvironmentGPT UNEP and the Sustainable Development Goals Media Media Centre News, stories and speeches Thematic feeds Social media Get involved Even",
   "Advancing heat resilience and sustainable cooling together | UNEP - UN Environment Programme Skip to main content Utility Knowledge repository Data and apps Language English Search Image search Go to search menu close Open main menu Main navigation Who we are About us Meetings and governance Evaluation, policies and strategies Funding Data hubs Platforms and partnerships Contact us Where we work Africa Asia and the Pacific Europe Latin America and the Caribbean North America and New York Office West Asia What we do Climate action Nature and land action Chemicals and pollution action Air quality Biosafety Cities Digital transformations Disasters and conflicts Energy Environment under review Environmental law and governance Extractives Finance and economic transformations Food systems Forests Fresh water Gender Ocean, seas and coasts Peatlands Sustainable Development Goals Transport Youth, education and environment What s new News, stories & speeches Thematic feeds Publications Events Get involved Agenda 2030 Breadcrumb Home News, stories & speeches Speeches Advancing heat resilience and sustainable cooling together Title Advancing heat resilience and sustainable cooling together Main image Image Speech Delivered By Inger Andersen Event Name Third Annual Cooling Ministerial: Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Location Antalya, Türkiye Published on 16 Nov 2026 Body Your Excellency Grace Fu, Minister for Sustainability and the Environment, Singapore, Murat Kurum, Minister of Environment, Urbanisation and Climate Change, Türkiye, Dr. Amna bint Abdullah Al Dahak, Minister of Climate Change and Environment, UAE, Adalberto Maluf, National Secretary of Urban Environment, Water Resources and Environmental Quality, Brazil, Ministers, excellencies, colleagues and friends. Welcome to the third Annual Cooling Ministerial, which follows on from the Global Cooling Pledge Assembly in Singapore. We are here, during COP31, to kickstart the development of a Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap. We have good reason to do so. Extreme heat has already intensified and spread across the globe. Summer temperature records are toppling as fast as trees in a wildfire. People are dying and falling ill. Productivity is dropping. Infrastructure is under stress. Budgets are being pinched ever tighter. And, with overshoot of 1.5°C locked in, things will get worse before they get better. If warming reaches 2°C, around 40 per cent of humanity could be exposed to extreme heat by 2050. So, we must pick up the pace on cutting greenhouse gas emissions as part of efforts to keep peak temperatures below 2°C and return to 1.5°C by the end of the century. But we must also build resilience to the extreme heat that cannot be avoided. Unfortunately, vulnerability is rising faster than preparedness. Nearly two billion people lack access to adequate cooling. And we are facing a growing energy crisis. Cooling already accounts for 20 per cent of global electricity consumption. Demand is rising and will even outpace power-hungry data centres in terms of growth. This will overload power systems, constrain energy security and push up consumer bills – not to mention increase greenhouse gas emissions, if the new cooling is not sustainable. This all points to one conclusion: heat resilience and sustainable cooling must advance together. Excellencies, This can be done. The Global Cooling Watch demonstrated that adaptation objectives can be met alongside mitigation objectives. The solutions are there. Passive and nature-based cooling. Low-energy and hybrid solutions that combine fans with air conditioning. Efficient cooling equipment, cold chains and district cooling. Climate-friendly refrigerants, through the Kigali Amendment. Deploying such solutions on a sustainable cooling pathway could deliver nearly two-thirds of available emissions cuts. Protect 3.5 billion additional people from heat-related risks by 2050. Improve food security, health, productivity and the liveability of cities. Save approximately US$1 trillion annually in end-user bills by 2050. Reduce peak electricity demand and strengthen energy security. The Cool Coalition and the Global Cooling P"
