   "COP31 Governments Back Action on Extreme Heat, Cooling | Mirage News National World Canada New Zealand UK Local Australia Government Local Council Police News Business Technology Science Education Life Health Social Travel Sport University News Timeline Menu ☰ Latest Protect Penalty Rates For Public Sector Two Transcription Factors Mimic Viral Protein's Benefits South West Rocks Businesses Unite to Support Community Storms Forecast for Central and Northern Australia Detectives Release CCTV Over Carrum Downs Car Fire Vietnam Turns to AI and Digitalization to Boost Productivity and Jobs Digital Health Record Bill Tops $500m, Double Original Estimate Emergency Services Levy Reform Back to Drawing Board New Sewerage Infrastructure Project to Future-Proof System Police Are Responding To Serious Crash - Mandogalup Mirage News Mirage News Mirage News World 18 Nov 2026 3:50 am AEDT Date Time Share COP31 Governments Back Action on Extreme Heat, Cooling As global temperatures rise, cooling must expand to reach nearly three billion people who could lack access by 2050, while exclusive reliance on air conditioning could drive emissions to 7.2 billion tonnes of CO e and strain electricity grids. Singapore, Brazil, Japan, and the United Kingdom commit to addressing both extreme heat and sustainable cooling through the Global Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap Over US$ 20 million in new support announced to accelerate sustainable cooling, strengthen heat resilience and implement the Global Cooling Pledge. Anatalya, 17 November 2026 - Countries attending the UN Climate Change Conference (COP31)(link is external) in Antalya, T rkiye, today backed an integrated global response to extreme heat and growing cooling needs, welcoming the Antalya Communiqu and Global Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap to protect people as global temperature rise gets set to exceed 1.5 C. Meeting at the 3 rd Cooling Pledge Ministerial(link is external) , co-chaired by Singapore and the UN Environment Programme (UNEP), ministers and high-level representatives outlined priorities for translating global commitments into action, including strengthening national and local cooling plans, scaling sustainable cooling solutions and mobilizing finance. Countries and partners also announced over US$ 20 million in new support to accelerate implementation of the Global Cooling Pledge. \"Nearly two billion people, globally, still lack adequate cooling,\" said Inger Andersen, Executive Director of the UN Environment Programme (UNEP). \"At the same time, growing demand for cooling is placing unprecedented pressure on electricity systems and contributing to greenhouse gas emissions. \"We need to reduce heat exposure and cooling demand through better planning, shade, trees and building design, while ensuring access to efficient, affordable and sustainable cooling and cleaner refrigeration,\" she added. \"The Communiqu sets the momentum, and the roadmap will help countries bring these solutions together.\" The Antalya Communiqu The Antalya Communiqu (link is external) sets out the next steps to bring together heat resilience and sustainable cooling. It aligns countries and partners around five priority areas where collective action, policy, finance, technology can transform markets and translate the Global Cooling Pledge in delivery for both heat resilience and sustainable cooling. It builds on the outcomes and priority action areas of the first Global Cooling Pledge Assembly in Singapore and includes developing a Global Heat Resilience and Sustainable Cooling Roadmap(link is external) ; advancing national and subnational cooling action plans; scaling up passive, nature-based, efficient and innovative cooling solutions; mobilizing finance, including through Article 6 of the Paris Agreement(link is external) ; and strengthening the science, political coordination, and international advocacy needed to accelerate implementation. The Roadmap Countries want to take the next step in dealing with extreme heat and boosting sustainable cooling. What they need now is a clear picture of heat risk and cooling demand: who is most exposed, which measures will protect them, and what those measures mean for the power grid and the climate. The Roadmap(link is external) is that step. The roadmap is a country-led and voluntary process, which will support countries in identifying the people, places and systems most exposed to extreme heat - assessing institutions and policy gaps, prioritising solutions suited to national circumstances, and identifying pathways to finance and implement them. \"No country can tackle rising heat alone. Singapore is committed to build on the momentum from the Global Cooling Pledge Assembly held in September this year to drive global action on heat and cooling,\" said Grace Fu, Singapore's Minister for Sustainability and the Environment. \"Through our upcoming ASEAN Chairmanship and our role as a Cool Leader globally, we will work with partners to share practical solutions, strengthen cooperation, and develop cooler and more resilient cities. This includes working with UNEP Cool Coalition and over 200 international partners to help shape the Global Heat Resilience & Sustainable Cooling Roadmap.\" At the ministerial, leaders emphasized the need to build on the science of the Global Cooling Watch report and the commitments made through the Global Cooling Pledge(link is external) and Beat the Heat(link is external) to make heat resilience and sustainable action an investment and action priority. Countries and partners announced over US$20 million in new funding support to the UNEP-led Cool Coalition to accelerate implementation of the Global Cooling Pledge. This includes expanded support through EPIC Deploy (Enabling Pledge Implementation for Cooling(link is external) ), supported by Italy's Ministry of the Environment and Energy Security (MASE)(link is external) , a UNEP initiative helping countries implement their cooling commitments by developing investment-ready projects and mobilizing finance to scale sustainable cooling solutions. Additional funding will support the adoption of efficient cooling technologies using climate-friendly refrigerants in major markets across the Global South, including Brazil, China, ASEAN and South Asia. These efforts will connect large-scale aggregated demand with manufacturers through the UNEP Cool Coalition Manufacturers Platform. Additional funding was also announced to strengthen national and subnational implementation of the Global Cooling Pledge commitments in signatory countries and cities and to advance heat resilience and sustainable cooling in the Horn of Africa. Funding partners include the Children's Investment Fund Foundation (CIFF), ClimateWorks Foundation, the Hewlett Foundation, the Sequoia Climate Foundation, the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC) and the UN Development Account. To sustain momentum beyond COP31, Japan announced that it will host the 2028 GlobalABC Annual Assembly(link is external) and Global Cooling Pledge Assembly(link is external) in Tokyo as a joint \"COOL + BUILD\" gathering. Bringing together the buildings and cooling communities, the event will strengthen international cooperation to accelerate the transition to energy-efficient, low-carbon and climate-resilient buildings, while expanding access to sustainable cooling and protecting people from extreme heat. /Public Release. This material from the originating organization/author(s) might be of the point-in-time nature, and edited for clarity, style and length. Mirage.News does not take institutional positions or sides, and all views, positions, and conclusions expressed herein are solely those of the author(s).View in full here . Why? Well, unlike many news organisations, we have no sponsors, no corporate or ideological interests. We don't put up a paywall – we believe in free access to information of public inter"
